! index_after_level.strap -- an I-box instruction that writes an index
! register a pending look-ahead level is indexed by.
!
! A level's addresses are derived when it is loaded (rl_prepare), but the
! E-box re-derived the effective address from the CURRENT index registers
! when it dispatched the level.  An index instruction run ahead of the
! level therefore moved its operand.  Found 2026-11-15 by the 7030 STRAP II
! MAIN test's driver (step 4b-i): "ST(BU,64),9.0($9)" was loaded with its
! word computed, then "V+I,$9,11.0" ran ahead of it, and the store landed
! in the NEXT record, so every record showed its predecessor's value.  It
! showed only for layouts where the store's level was still pending, which
! is why each case here is repeated with 0, 1 and 2 extra pending loads.
!
! THE FIX, SECOND FORM (2026-11-15): the level carries the I-box's
! indexing (StretchTypes' LaCarry, StretchIBox's carryIndex/carriedInstr)
! and the E-box indexes nothing, so the V+I runs ahead WITHOUT waiting, as
! on the 7030, whose levels hold no address or index field (Buchholz
! §15.3, printed pp.238-240; R23-9732 Fig. 3.2.2, PDF 93).  The first form,
! commit 3204601, made the index write wait (levelIndexedBy); that wait is
! gone, and the CYCLES window on this deck's run_tests.sh line is what
! says so: 2721 clocks with it, 2407 without.  The E-box sibling -- a
! level storing into $X3 ahead of a level indexed by $3 -- is a real
! dependence and still waits: first through rl_prepare's
! indexStorePending, since 2026-11-16 through the broader STORE WAIT
! (StretchIBox's storeWait; decks/index_after_index_store.strap).
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from the sequential programming
! model, not transcribed.  Expected values are worked out by hand from it.
! Each case puts a slow level ahead (a store, then a load of the same word,
! which waits for it) so the indexed level is still pending when the next
! instruction is prepared.
!
! OBSERVED, 2026-11-15, the carried form, on commit 5adbbb4 (before the
! store wait; the four bsv files each restored
! from a copy checked with cmp; run with this deck's run_tests.sh line):
!   E-box re-indexes (rl_au_remove dispatches h.di, not the carried
!     instruction; the stall already gone): FAIL DV0a DV0b DV1a DV1b DV2a
!     AE0 and cycles (2406) only -- the same value set the stall's removal
!     failed under the first form.
!   indexStorePending always False (before the store wait replaced it):
!     FAIL GE0 and DCa only.  So the wait
!     discriminates on one E case of three.  Why GE1 and GE2 pass without
!     it, and why C's ST(BU,64),0($3) then misses DC, was NOT TRACED.  One
!     untested reading: the next case's LVI,$3 overtakes E's pending
!     ST(BU,64),$X3 (the open I-box hazard in bsv/README.md), the late
!     store leaves $3 = AB, and E1/E2 read AB by coincidence.  If so, this
!     wait is also shielding that hazard in this deck.
!   storeWait never holds (2026-11-16, the store wait in place of
!     indexStorePending): FAIL GE0 and DCa only, the same set.
!   The old stall put back (levelIndexedBy on the immediate-index arm):
!     FAIL cycles = 2552 only -- every value right, the clocks wrong.
! OBSERVED, 2026-11-15, the first form (stall), for the record:
!   levelIndexedBy always False: FAIL DV0a DV0b DV1a DV1b DV2a and AE0;
!   indexStorePending always False: FAIL GE0 GE1 GE2.
! NOT SHOWN TO DISCRIMINATE: L0-L2 (LV), S0-S2 (SV into $X3), F0, F1 and C
! (SIC into $3, whose B drains the levels anyway) pass with the mechanism
! off: on this model those instructions do not get ahead of the level here.
! They stay as guards, since the sequential model requires the same result.
!
!
! TIMING RE-PINNED 2026-11-16, 2407 -> 2828 clocks, every value check unchanged.
! VFL E-box time now depends on the operation: bsv/StretchTiming.bsv's
! eboxClocks charges each operation the throughput Perf. Char. Vol. 2's
! atlas prints for it (docs/atlas_oracle.md).  This deck's VFL loads and
! stores, full-word (BU,64), went from 24 E-box clocks each to L 28 (PDF
! 120, 8.40 us) and ST 32 (PDF 124, 9.61 us).  The 2721 / 2552 figures
! above were measured under the old timing and were not re-measured.
!
! TIMING RE-PINNED 2026-11-17, 2828 -> 2864 clocks, every value check unchanged.
! A store into index storage now holds decoding until the store is done,
! not until its level is removed: Perf. Char. Vol. 1 printed p.3-21, "the
! I-box does no further decoding until the new index contents arrives"
! (bsv/StretchCore.bsv xstoreFreeAt, set by laarRetire to the E-box time
! plus the 0.6 us check, StretchTiming's laarCheckClocks).  The model
! commits a level's result at removal and charges its E-box time after,
! so the old release came that E-box time early.
! The SV and ST into $X3 of its cases.  MEASURED: with that hold removed
! (and nothing else) the deck runs in 2828 clocks again.
! CYCLES 2864 -> 2854, 2026-11-17: the preparation floor (StretchCore's `ib`
! and rl_prepare's load clock; docs/atlas_oracle.md "Closing the
! residuals" item 7).  With that mechanism removed the run is 2864 again;
! no value moved.
        SLC, (8)2000.0
START   NOP
! ---- V0: V+I, $3, 1.0, 0 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DV0
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RV0
! ---- V1: V+I, $3, 1.0, 1 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DV1
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RV1
! ---- V2: V+I, $3, 1.0, 2 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DV2
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RV2
! ---- L0: LV, $3, PTRX, 0 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DL0
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        LV, $3, PTRX
        SV, $3, RL0
! ---- L1: LV, $3, PTRX, 1 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DL1
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        LV, $3, PTRX
        SV, $3, RL1
! ---- L2: LV, $3, PTRX, 2 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DL2
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        LV, $3, PTRX
        SV, $3, RL2
! ---- S0: SV, $5, $X3, 0 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DS0
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        SV, $5, $X3
        SV, $3, RS0
! ---- S1: SV, $5, $X3, 1 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DS1
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        SV, $5, $X3
        SV, $3, RS1
! ---- S2: SV, $5, $X3, 2 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DS2
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        SV, $5, $X3
        SV, $3, RS2
! ---- F0: a floating ST level, V+I, 0 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DF0
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L, SRC
        ST, 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RF0
! ---- F1: a floating ST level, V+I, 1 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DF1
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L, SRC
        ST, 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RF1
! ---- F2: a floating ST level, V+I, 2 extra pending load(s)
        LVI, $3, DF2
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L, SRC
        ST, 0($3)
        V+I, $3, 1.0
        SV, $3, RF2
! ---- E0: a level stores into $X3, the next level is indexed by $3, 0
        LVI, $3, AE0
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), XE0
        ST(BU,64), $X3
        L(BU,64), 0($3)
        ST(BU,64), GE0
! ---- E1: a level stores into $X3, the next level is indexed by $3, 1
        LVI, $3, AE1
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), XE1
        ST(BU,64), $X3
        L(BU,64), 0($3)
        ST(BU,64), GE1
! ---- E2: a level stores into $X3, the next level is indexed by $3, 2
        LVI, $3, AE2
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), XE2
        ST(BU,64), $X3
        L(BU,64), 0($3)
        ST(BU,64), GE2
! ---- C: SIC into $3 (the SIC,$15 shape), last because a wrong store
! ---- lands in the code
        LVI, $3, DC
        L(BU,64), SRC
        ST(BU,64), TMP
        L(BU,64), TMP
        ST(BU,64), 0($3)
        SIC, $3
        B, CNEXT
CNEXT   SV, $3, RC
        L(BU,64), DC
        ST(BU,64), GC
DONE    B, DONE
        CNOP
SRC     DD(BU,64), (16)5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A
TMP     DD(BU,64), 0
PTRX    DD(BU,64), (16)0000400000000000
AB      DD(BU,64), (16)BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB
DV0      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RV0      DD(BU,64), 0
DV1      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RV1      DD(BU,64), 0
DV2      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RV2      DD(BU,64), 0
DL0      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RL0      DD(BU,64), 0
DL1      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RL1      DD(BU,64), 0
DL2      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RL2      DD(BU,64), 0
DS0      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RS0      DD(BU,64), 0
DS1      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RS1      DD(BU,64), 0
DS2      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RS2      DD(BU,64), 0
DF0      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RF0      DD(BU,64), 0
DF1      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RF1      DD(BU,64), 0
DF2      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RF2      DD(BU,64), 0
AE0      DD(BU,64), (16)AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
XE0      XW, AB
GE0      DD(BU,64), 0
AE1      DD(BU,64), (16)AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
XE1      XW, AB
GE1      DD(BU,64), 0
AE2      DD(BU,64), (16)AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
XE2      XW, AB
GE2      DD(BU,64), 0
DC      DD(BU,64), 0
        DD(BU,64), 0
RC      DD(BU,64), 0
GC      DD(BU,64), 0
        END, START

